---
/**
 * foodcraft / TrustBL — "Kalinaw Coffee"'s proof strip.
 *
 * ROLE. The espresso panel the design drops 62px under the hero: a rounded
 * (16px) dark slab sitting INSIDE the 1240 wrap — not full-bleed — carrying a
 * four-up grid of centred cells. Each cell is a 30px AMBER Fraunces figure over
 * a 10px tracked-caps label, with a hairline between the columns. It is the
 * first thing read after the headline and the band most likely to be believed,
 * so not one word in it is written by this component.
 *
 * EDITABLE HOOKS (both declared by components/editor/genericContentSchema.ts
 * under the `trust.cells` list spec — live in the sidebar with add / remove /
 * reorder, maxItems 4):
 *   trust.cells.${i}.num    — the amber figure. set:html, so an owner's
 *                             "&lt; 1 hr", "24/7", "₱—" or "6am–6pm" survives
 *                             escaping.
 *   trust.cells.${i}.label  — the tracked-caps label under it.
 * There is no other hook here. A data-field on any path that schema does not
 * declare is a dead hook that no sidebar input owns.
 *
 * DERIVED (computed, never authored by this file):
 *   · `rows` — the cells that actually carry text, each carrying its ORIGINAL
 *     array index `i` so a data-field always addresses the real slot. Dropping
 *     a blank cell 2 must never redirect an edit of cell 3 into it (R6). The
 *     stagger delay uses the DISPLAY index instead, because it is decoration.
 *   · `--cols` — the column count follows the DATA, capped at the design's
 *     four. A hardcoded repeat(4) would strand two empty tracks on a two-fact
 *     panel and shove the content into the left half of the slab.
 *
 * NOT RENDERED, and why:
 *   · NO eyebrow, NO headline, NO lede, NO button, NO photo. The design's TRUST
 *     panel has none of them and the schema declares none for `trust`.
 *   · NO placeholder figure, NO zero, NO em dash. The demo's numbers are the
 *     DESIGNER filling their own template; a bean-origin count or a roast date
 *     is a claim only the café may make. lib/derive-content-defaults.ts carries
 *     no `trust` key at all, so this panel exists only where a human typed.
 *   · NO section at all when nothing was typed (R5). The gate counts BODY
 *     content — cells with real text — not a tag or a headline, because this
 *     band has neither to miscount. lib/astro-builder.ts already forces
 *     visibility.trustBlock false on a zero-length array; this gate is the belt
 *     to that brace, catching what the builder cannot see: rows that are
 *     PRESENT but blank, since the editor's Add button inserts
 *     { num: '', label: '' } and the builder gates on raw array LENGTH.
 *   · NO onPage() anchor-remap helper, and that is deliberate rather than an
 *     oversight: this panel emits no <a> at all, so PAGE_ANCHORS / ANCHOR_ALIAS
 *     would be unreachable dead code in this one file. The remap belongs in
 *     every BL section that does emit an href (menu / about / gallery /
 *     location / faq / main). TrustBK made the same call for the same reason.
 *   · NO role="img", NO plate, NO cap — there is no image slot in this panel, so
 *     there is nothing to name (R9). Nothing here is aria-hidden either: the
 *     figure and the label are both real owner content and are announced
 *     normally, and there is no decorative numeral or icon to suppress.
 *   · NO star row, NO rating — this band carries no review, so R2's rating rule
 *     has nothing to bind to here.
 *
 * DECISIONS a reader would otherwise take for a mistake:
 *
 *   · `band-dark` sits on the PANEL, not on the <section>. The design paints
 *     #241A12 on the rounded inner div inside a 1240/32 gutter, and leaves the
 *     page cream on either side of it. Putting the class on the section would
 *     flood the full viewport width with espresso — a different drawing. Every
 *     band-dark rule in PageBL is a DESCENDANT selector, so it reaches the fig,
 *     the mono and the focus ring from the panel just as well.
 *
 *   · The FIGURE is overridden to --band-accent. `:where(.band-dark) .fig` gives
 *     --bone, which is the wrapper's sensible default for a figure on espresso;
 *     the design's trust numerals are specifically #E8A33C, the same amber it
 *     uses for the band eyebrows and the band button. That is the design's
 *     accent-on-espresso and it is reproduced, not re-derived. amber/band is
 *     7.91:1. The override needs to beat (0,2,1), so it is written as a
 *     descendant of .kc-proof-slab — a bare .kc-proof-fig compiles to (0,2,0)
 *     under this project's `scopedStyleStrategy: "attribute"` and would silently
 *     lose.
 *
 *   · The LABEL is overridden to --bone-soft, and this is the one value in the
 *     file that is NOT an exact token match, so here is the whole reasoning.
 *     The design's label is #A2907E (5.54:1 on the band). PageBL declares no
 *     token with that value — it is an outlier the designer also reuses on the
 *     CREAM footer bar, a generic quiet-caps colour rather than a band colour.
 *     The candidates, and why:
 *        --muted      #948373  nearest in raw RGB (Δ22), and it DOES clear AA
 *                              on espresso: 4.67:1, where the bar for a 10px
 *                              label is 4.5. Contrast is not what rules it out.
 *                              The THEME ENGINE is. --muted is the cream-ground
 *                              token and lib/genericThemeOverrides.ts rewrites
 *                              it — `--muted: palette.inkSoft !important` — to
 *                              a muted ink picked against the SCHEME'S PAPER,
 *                              so a light scheme prints a near-black label on
 *                              the espresso slab. It is safe on cream, and only
 *                              on cream.
 *        --bone-soft  #B8A795  the band's own quiet-text token, Δ39, one notch
 *                              LIGHTER than the design's value, 7.31:1 on the
 *                              band — and it is in the group that override list
 *                              leaves alone (--bone and its two soft variants
 *                              are never rewritten), so it survives any scheme.
 *        --bone-soft-2 #CDBFAD what `:where(.band-dark) .mono` already gives —
 *                              Δ79, clearly lighter than drawn, and it flattens
 *                              the design's deliberate step down from the amber
 *                              figure to the quiet label.
 *     --bone-soft is the closest of the two BAND-SAFE tokens and it keeps the
 *     figure/label hierarchy the design draws. If the
 *     owner's palette is ever given a token at #A2907E, this is a one-line
 *     change. No literal hex is introduced either way (R1).
 *
 *   · An owner's <em> inside `num` is LEFT ITALIC — the opposite of what
 *     TrustBK does. There the display face (Prata) ships no italic, so an <em>
 *     only synthesised an oblique digit. Fraunces ships a real italic at 500
 *     and the italic emphasis IS this template's signature, so it is allowed to
 *     stand. It inherits AMBER rather than picking up the page's rust `.em`
 *     colour, because rust on espresso is 1.6:1 and would delete the emphasised
 *     characters — and it inherits it for free, since `:where(.h2 em)` and
 *     `:where(.em)` cannot reach a bare <em> inside a .fig. No rule is written,
 *     so no :global() is needed; had one been, set:html output sits outside
 *     scoped CSS and would have required it (R3).
 *
 *   · The RIGHT-HAND hairline on the LAST cell is the design's. Its sc-for puts
 *     border-right on every repeated cell, so the strip closes with a short
 *     vertical rule at the panel edge. Reproduced verbatim rather than
 *     "tidied" into a border-left-except-first, which is a different drawing.
 *     `overflow: hidden` on the panel is what the design's border-radius
 *     implies once that trailing rule exists: without it the hairline runs
 *     straight past the 16px corner curve and prints two dark ticks on the
 *     cream page above and below the slab. Nothing in this band is focusable,
 *     so the clip cannot swallow a focus ring.
 *
 *   · The section's padding is `62px 0 0`, not a margin. The design writes
 *     `margin:62px auto 0`; the section here has no ground of its own (the
 *     panel carries it), so padding is visually identical and cannot collapse
 *     out through <main> on a site whose hero block is switched off. The
 *     wrapper's shared `section { padding: clamp(56px,7.6vw,96px) 0 }` is
 *     (0,1,1) and a scoped class beats it without !important.
 *
 *   · The label's leading is PINNED at 1.3 rather than inherited. The design
 *     sets no line-height on its caps labels, so in the design document they
 *     compute to `normal`; PageBL's `body { line-height: 1.7 }` would otherwise
 *     inflate this 10px line box and print the design's 9px gap under the
 *     figure as ~13px, and the cell several px taller than drawn.
 *
 *   · The label's font-weight is NOT restated. .mono is already 10px / .2em /
 *     uppercase / var(--sans), which is the design's label treatment; its 500
 *     is the wrapper's tracked-caps weight and a section restating a primitive
 *     is how a family drifts apart.
 *
 * No id: nothing on the page links to this panel (the BL anchor set is menu,
 * about, gallery, location, faq, main).
 */
interface Props { content: any; layout?: any; }
const { content } = Astro.props;

const trust = content?.trust || {};
// Tolerate both shapes at every read: the schema's { cells: [...] } and the
// legacy plain array some older records still store under content.trust.
const cells: any[] = Array.isArray(trust.cells)
  ? trust.cells
  : (Array.isArray(trust) ? trust : []);

const asText = (v: any) => (v === null || v === undefined ? '' : String(v));

// The ORIGINAL index rides along in `i`. Only rows with real text survive, so a
// half-filled row prints one line and an empty row prints no cell at all — no
// hairline dividing a column that is not there.
const rows = cells
  .map((c: any, i: number) => ({ num: asText(c?.num), label: asText(c?.label), i }))
  .filter((r) => Boolean(r.num.trim() || r.label.trim()));

// Follow the data, but never widen past the design's four tracks (the schema
// caps the list at 4 anyway). Anything beyond four wraps to a second row and
// picks up a horizontal hairline above it.
const cols = String(Math.min(Math.max(rows.length, 1), 4));
---
{rows.length > 0 && (
  <section class="kc-proof">
    <div class="wrap">
      <div class="kc-proof-slab band-dark" style={`--cols:${cols}`}>
        {rows.map((r, k) => (
          <div class="kc-proof-cell reveal" data-delay={String(k % 4)}>
            {r.num.trim() && (
              <div class="fig kc-proof-fig" data-field={`trust.cells.${r.i}.num`} set:html={r.num}></div>
            )}
            {r.label.trim() && (
              <div class="mono kc-proof-lab" data-field={`trust.cells.${r.i}.label`}>{r.label}</div>
            )}
          </div>
        ))}
      </div>
    </div>
  </section>
)}
<style>
  /* Colour and type come only from the wrapper's token vocabulary
     (--band --band-rule --band-accent --bone-soft --disp --sans --r-lg, via
     .band-dark / .fig / .mono). There is not one literal hex in this file. */

  /* The design's TRUST section has no vertical padding of its own — the cells
     do all of it — and it sits 62px under the hero. See the frontmatter note on
     why that 62px is padding rather than the design's margin. */
  .kc-proof { padding: 62px 0 0; }

  /* The rounded espresso slab. --r-lg IS the design's 16px; .band-dark supplies
     the ground and, by descent, the fig / mono / focus treatments. */
  .kc-proof-slab {
    border-radius: var(--r-lg);
    /* The design's border-radius only reads as a radius once the trailing
       hairline is clipped to it — see the frontmatter note. */
    overflow: hidden;
    display: grid;
    grid-template-columns: repeat(var(--cols, 1), minmax(0, 1fr));
  }

  /* 32px 22px, centred, with the design's hairline on the right of EVERY cell,
     including the last, which closes the strip at the panel edge. */
  .kc-proof-cell {
    min-width: 0;
    padding: 32px 22px;
    text-align: center;
    border-right: 1px solid var(--band-rule);
  }
  /* Only reachable if a record predates the schema's maxItems 4 cap. */
  .kc-proof-cell:nth-child(n + 5) { border-top: 1px solid var(--band-rule); }

  /* .fig already gives Fraunces 400, tabular lining numerals and line-height 1.
     The design's size is a flat 30px and its colour is the band's amber, not
     the wrapper's default bone for a figure on espresso — the descendant
     selector is what beats that (0,2,1) rule. */
  .kc-proof-slab .kc-proof-fig {
    font-size: 30px;
    color: var(--band-accent);
    /* A pasted URL or an unspaced string must not push the page sideways. */
    overflow-wrap: anywhere;
  }

  /* .mono is already 10px / .2em / uppercase / var(--sans), exactly the design's
     label treatment. Three things are local:
       · colour — the design's quiet label is DARKER than the wrapper's default
         for .mono on a band; see the frontmatter note for the full reasoning
         and the two rejected candidates.
       · margin-top: 9px — the design's gap under the figure.
       · line-height: 1.3 — the design's caps labels set no leading, so they
         compute to `normal` in the design document. PageBL's body 1.7 would
         leak in, inflate this 10px line box and print that 9px gap as ~13px.
         1.3 restores the design's rhythm and still lets a longer owner label
         wrap without the two lines' cap-heights touching. */
  .kc-proof-slab .kc-proof-lab {
    color: var(--bone-soft);
    margin-top: 9px;
    line-height: 1.3;
    overflow-wrap: anywhere;
  }

  /* ── This section's OWN responsive pass. The wrapper handles the shared
        760 / 520 type pass; the grid below is ours (R8). ─────────────────── */
  @media (max-width: 900px) {
    /* Two up. The interior vertical rule is now the odd cell's right border and
       the second pair gains a horizontal one. Every rule is restated rather
       than left to lapse — (0,2,0) beats (0,1,0) whatever the source order. */
    .kc-proof-slab { grid-template-columns: repeat(2, minmax(0, 1fr)); }
    .kc-proof-cell:nth-child(n + 3) { border-top: 1px solid var(--band-rule); }
  }
  @media (max-width: 520px) {
    /* One column. A 10px label at .2em tracking cannot hold a ~180px half
       column without splitting mid-word. The trailing right hairline is dropped
       here: at full width it would draw a stray vertical line down the panel
       edge instead of dividing anything.

       ONLY the VERTICAL padding moves off the design's 32px 22px, and only
       because stacking multiplies it: four cells one above the other spend the
       design's vertical padding four times over. The 22px HORIZONTAL padding is
       the design's and stays — nothing at this width is pressing on it (the
       wrap is 92vw, so the cell is ~390px wide).

       The figure stays at the design's flat 30px. It is NOT shrunk: the cell is
       WIDER one-up at 520 than two-up at 900, where the figure is left alone,
       so a 520-only reduction would be both a deviation and backwards. Long
       unbroken strings are already handled by overflow-wrap above. */
    .kc-proof-slab { grid-template-columns: minmax(0, 1fr); }
    .kc-proof-cell {
      padding: 24px 22px;
      border-right: 0;
      border-top: 1px solid var(--band-rule);
    }
    .kc-proof-cell:first-child { border-top: 0; }
  }
</style>
